---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import { paperTrailsCaseStudy } from '../../data/case-studies';

const sectionLinks = [
    { href: '#rationale', label: 'Why rebuild?' },
    { href: '#decision', label: 'The decision' },
    { href: '#scope', label: 'Scope and delivery' },
    { href: '#release', label: 'Release and compatibility' },
    { href: '#outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
    { href: '#lessons', label: 'What I learned' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout study={paperTrailsCaseStudy} sectionLinks={sectionLinks}>
    <Fragment slot="intro">
        I co-founded Paper Trails with my brother and run its technical side. In late 2025, I rebuilt our
        React frontend and PHP API as one Next.js and TypeScript application. We launched in early January
        2026, ahead of our February target, after roughly three months of part-time implementation alongside
        my full-time job.
    </Fragment>
    <dl slot="facts" class="facts">
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Technical Director and Co-Founder</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Migration</dt>
            <dd>React / PHP API to Next.js / TypeScript</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Launch</dt>
            <dd>Early January 2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Transition</dt>
            <dd>Less than one hour of downtime</dd>
        </div>
    </dl>
    <section class="section" id="rationale">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 / Why rebuild?</p>
            <h2>A working product that was becoming harder to change</h2>
            <p>
                Paper Trails turns outdoor activity data into personalised prints. Our frontend and PHP
                backend lived in separate repositories, and keeping them in step had become awkward as small
                bugs accumulated. Partner campaigns exposed another problem: adding a custom theme meant
                changing numerous files, making each release harder to publish and check.
            </p>
            <p>
                We reviewed analytics, order history and database logs to decide which features customers used
                enough to justify their maintenance cost. Success meant preserving the important customer
                journeys, making custom work easier and keeping previous order images accessible. A possible
                partner campaign gave us a February 2026 target.
            </p>
            <p>
                I owned the architecture, technical decisions, implementation and delivery planning. My
                brother contributed branding, marketing and theme design, and helped agree the reduced scope
                and customer experience.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="decision">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 / The decision</p>
            <h2>One application to build and release</h2>
            <p>
                I considered refactoring the existing PHP application. It already worked, and replacing it
                meant spending time on a rewrite, changing our deployment setup and risking regressions. But
                I'd over-engineered the original architecture. Refactoring would still leave me maintaining
                two applications in different languages.
            </p>
            <p>
                I'd already used Next.js at Quilter. It gave me a familiar way to bring the client and server
                code together, with shared TypeScript models, schemas and validation. Server actions replaced
                our separate application REST API. Server-side validation and third-party credentials still
                belonged on the server, and external integrations and webhooks remained part of the system.
            </p>
            <p>
                We kept much of the database structure and brought existing test cases into the replacement.
                We also moved checkout to Stripe's hosted payment flow, reducing the payment code we
                maintained ourselves.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="scope">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">03 / Scope and delivery</p>
            <h2>Keep the first release focused</h2>
            <p>
                My brother and I agreed the must-haves before implementation. We removed preloaded example
                activities that customers sometimes ordered by mistake, starting with an empty map instead. We
                also removed automatic print labels, which created support work around differing units and
                inconsistent activity data. The old feature backlog stayed outside the launch requirements.
            </p>
            <p>
                I used technical design documents for major features and domains to break the work into
                smaller GitHub issues beneath parent issues. Labels, milestones and a Kanban board helped me
                track progress across several months of part-time work.
            </p>
            <p>
                AI agents helped organise the work and draft much of the implementation. They updated issues
                and supplied test results, screenshots and browser reproductions. I checked the customer
                experience myself, using manual checks and Storybook to inspect themes and component
                variations.
            </p>
            <p>
                I put developer tooling and tests first, then worked through the application structure, print
                creation, checkout and integrations. Checking features as we went helped keep the work
                manageable.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="release">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">04 / Release and compatibility</p>
            <h2>Keep old orders accessible and a way back</h2>
            <p>
                Before launch, I worked through a deployment checklist on staging and a production release
                candidate. I retained database, storage and repository backups, and used analytics to choose a
                quiet period for the domain switch.
            </p>
            <p>
                The transition included moving existing order data and images and took less than an hour of
                downtime. Customers could still retrieve previous print images using their existing order IDs.
                ID prefixes distinguished versions and routed requests to the appropriate images.
            </p>
            <p>
                After switching the domain, we made a live payment ourselves and watched for errors. The old
                application stayed operational with the data and integrations needed to take orders. If
                verification failed, the plan was to reverse the domain switch and investigate the logs. This
                was a manual rollback plan.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="outcome">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">05 / Outcome</p>
            <h2>Launched ahead of the February target</h2>
            <p>
                We went live in early January 2026 after approximately three months of part-time
                implementation in late 2025. Custom themes now involve only a few files. Shared models and
                validation reduce duplicated logic, and we spend less time switching repositories and
                coordinating separate releases.
            </p>
            <p>
                GitHub Actions runs the checks, and a merge to main deploys the application together on
                DigitalOcean. The improvement is in maintenance and release coordination. I haven't measured a
                before-and-after time saving or a revenue or conversion change.
            </p>
            <p>
                After launch, we added an order dashboard that brings payment, email and fulfilment events
                into a traceable history. Better logging also helped us diagnose memory growth during
                on-demand image processing. We addressed that by generating optimised images ahead of
                deployment.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="lessons">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">06 / What I learned</p>
            <h2>Check the whole customer journey earlier</h2>
            <p>
                I'd introduced complexity for possibilities that never became important. Actual usage gave us
                a better basis for deciding what to keep.
            </p>
            <p>
                I'd also verify smaller changes through the complete ordering flow earlier. Breaking work into
                small GitHub tasks wasn't enough if the checks stopped at the boundaries between features.
            </p>
            <p>
                A post-launch review found gaps in our end-to-end tests around the return from Stripe
                checkout. Unfinished print configurations lived in browser storage, making retrieval after
                payment dependent on browser state and difficult to test across browsers. I introduced
                temporary MongoDB records with a 72-hour expiry so the configuration could be retrieved
                independently of the browser. The trigger was the test review, rather than a reported customer
                payment failure.
            </p>
            <p>
                Next time, I'd check something as specific as a print's orientation all the way through
                checkout and into the completed order before expanding to the other customisation options.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a separate production incident, read the
                <a href="/case-studies/ai-engineering/">Paper Trails checkout case study</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
    .facts {
        display: grid;
        margin-top: 48px;
        border-top: 2px solid var(--site-border);
        border-left: 2px solid var(--site-border);
    }

    .fact {
        min-width: 0;
        padding: 20px;
        border-right: 2px solid var(--site-border);
        border-bottom: 2px solid var(--site-border);
    }
</style>
